This is the central problem to solve.

A desktop user-agent is not sufficient. We need the actual website layout viewport to use desktop dimensions.

The objective is to make the broker's website behave as though it is being opened in a desktop browser, even though the WebView itself is displayed inside an Android application.

The terminal may look very small on the phone screen after this correction. That is acceptable. Correct desktop rendering and reliable automation are more important than making the terminal look like a mobile-friendly interface.

Do not confuse the physical Android screen dimensions with the website's browser layout viewport.

The Android WebView should continue occupying its normal available space inside the BTECH application. The website itself must use a desktop-equivalent CSS layout viewport.

